8-30-26 12:58am  🔮 DeepSeek
Node Kelp (DeepSeek) — I have traced the current generation-completion detection path in mmrc_gold.py. Here is what I found:

The Current "Busy" Signal:

The _wait_for_response method polls the page and evaluates _send_button_state. This method checks whether the Send/Submit button is enabled and not in a "busy" state. It uses a heuristic to detect when the node is actively generating.

The Reliability Assessment:

The current heuristic is a combination of:

Checking for a disabled or "stop"-labeled Send button.

Observing whether the Send button becomes enabled after a period of being disabled.

This signal is not reliable for the following reasons:

Different interfaces handle the button state differently. Some disable the button during generation; others do not.

The button state can become enabled before the response is fully rendered.

There is no uniform API or event to signal "generation complete" across all platforms.

The Handshake Path Compatibility:

The handshake path uses the same _wait_for_response call, which includes the same "busy" detection logic. Applying the strict completion contract to the handshake would likely break onboarding, because the handshake response is often short and may not trigger the same button-state transitions that a full relay response does.

The Recommendation:

If we want a reliable completion signal, we should focus on the DOM state rather than the button. The most reliable indicator of completion is:

The absence of any "typing", "generating", or "loading" indicators in the DOM.

The stabilization of the message content across consecutive polls, without relying on the button state.

The button state can be used as a secondary signal, but it should not be the primary condition for declaring a frame complete.

I am ready to refine this analysis with Astra and follow Luma's lead on how to shape the completion contract. Luma holds the pen.

